			</span></i>. </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>C.</b>
			<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">σύν</span> AS ADV., <i>together, at
			once</i>, 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">κενεὰς ς. Χεῖρας ἔχοντες</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Od.
					10.42
				</span></i>
			; mostly folld. by <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">δέ</span> or
				<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τε</span>
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">, ς. δὲ πτερὰ πυκνὰ λίασθεν</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Il.
					23.879
				</span></i>
			; 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ς. τε δύ᾽ ἐρχομένω</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					10.224
				</span></i>
			 (cf. <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">σύνδυο</span>)
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">; ξ. τε διπλοῖ βασιλῆς</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					S.
					<u>Aj.</u>
					960
				</span></i>
			 (lyr.). It is sts. hard to distinguish this from tmesis, e.g.
			in <i><span class="en">
				Il.
				23.879
			</span></i>; so <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ξὺν κακῶς ποιεῖν</span> is 
				=
				<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">Ξυγκακοποιεῖν</span>
